/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  filter02_caco3_pressWashing

  A PLATE-AND-FRAME PRESS WITH THROUGH-WASHING, held to the printed worked
  examples of Geankoplis, Hersel & Lepek (5th ed.), Examples 24.2-1, 24.2-2
  and 24.2-3, pp. 1083-1088: the CaCO3 slurry of the laboratory test (alpha
  = 1.863e11 m/kg, R_m = 10.63e10 1/m at 338 kN/m2, c_S = 23.47 kg/m3 of
  filtrate) in a 20-frame press of 17.46 m2; 3.37 m3 of filtrate per cycle;
  through-washing with wash water equal to 10 % of the filtrate; 20 min to
  clean.

  THE BOOK'S ANSWERS: K_p = 37.93 s/m6 and B = 16.10 s/m3 (p. 1086); the
    filtration time 269.7 s (eq. 24.2-17, p. 1086); the washing rate
    1.737e-3 m3/s and time 194.0 s (eq. 24.2-20, p. 1087); the cycle
    27.73 min (p. 1088).
  THE STEADY REPRESENTATION: the streams are cycle-averaged; the press's
    cycle and the number of presses that pass the average feed are results
    (docs/design/c361-solids-processing.md 8.3).  The feed is SET to what
    one press passes on the book's cycle (3.37 m3 of filtrate per 27.73 min,
    2.0255e-3 m3/s), so `pressesRequired` near 1 is a consistency check of
    the cycle arithmetic, not an anchor.  The wash flow is 10 % of the
    filtrate's, which makes 0.337 m3 per cycle as in the book.
  WHAT IS NOT THE BOOK'S:
    * m = 2.0 kg wet / kg dry: Examples 24.2-1 to -3 do not give the cake's
      moisture; Example 24.2-4 gives m = 2.0 for "a CaCO3 slurry as given in
      Example 24.2-1", and it is taken here.  With it the feed's c_x =
      c_S / (rho + m c_S) = 0.02248 reproduces c_S = 23.47.
    * the viscosity and density are the package's (Vogel 8.89e-4 Pa s against
      the book's 8.937e-4; K_p and B scale with it).
    * the solute left in the cake: water washes water here, so the washing
      law (`displacement`) has nothing to remove; the wash ratio N is shown.
      No book in the folder prints a washing-efficiency law with constants
      (design record 8.6).
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
application   choupoSolve;
description   "A plate-and-frame filter press with through-washing on Geankoplis's CaCO3 slurry (Examples 24.2-1 to 24.2-3): filtration time, washing time and cycle time on the cycle-averaged steady balance.";
verbosity     3;
citations
(
    "Geankoplis, C.J.; Hersel, A.A.; Lepek, D.H. Transport Processes and Separation Process Principles, 5th ed. Prentice Hall (year not read on the page). Section 24.2, eqs. 24.2-13 to 24.2-20, Examples 24.2-1 to 24.2-4, pp. 1078-1090."
    "Ruth, B.F. (1935) Studies in filtration III. Derivation of general filtration equations. Ind. Eng. Chem. 27(6), 708-723 (the two-resistance law as given by Geankoplis; the paper is in the literature folder and was not read for this case)."
);
